    {
      "title": "Item list endpoint returns every user's items to non-superusers (data leak)",
      "tcs": [
        "TC-017",
        "TC-043"
      ],
      "classification": "product_bug",
      "recommendation": "fix_code",
      "severity": "Sev1",
      "confidence": "high",
      "suspect_module": "backend/app/api/routes/items.py",
      "suspect_detail": "read_items(), non-superuser else-branch: the `.where(Item.owner_id == current_user.id)` filter was removed from both the count statement and the item select statement",
      "root_cause": "The diff deleted the owner_id filter in the non-superuser branch of GET /items/. That branch is now identical to the superuser branch, so any authenticated user gets all users' items and a global count.",
      "rationale": "Both failures trace directly to the changed lines. TC-017 shows extra item IDs belonging to other users. TC-043 shows user B not getting the empty state because A's item is returned. Nothing suggests this is a deliberate rule change, since the page copy says 'Create and manage your items' and the single-item endpoints still enforce ownership with 403. This is a cross-tenant data exposure. The frontend is not at fault because it correctly renders whatever the API returns.",
      "steps": [
        "Create user A and user B (non-superusers)",
        "As A, POST /api/v1/items/ twice; as B, POST once",
        "As A, GET /api/v1/items/",
        "Alternatively, in the UI log in as B, who has no items, and open /items"
      ],
      "expected": "A receives 200 with only A's 2 items and count=2. B sees the empty state 'You don't have any items yet'.",
      "actual": "The response includes items owned by other users, and count covers all items in the table. B sees A's items instead of the empty state.",
      "id": "DEF-001",
      "status": "Closed",
      "run_id": "run-20261116-150942",
      "regression_from": "aabb901",
      "evidence": [
        {
